messages:
alex: customer reported billing spike after retries — they see the same POST charged 3x in a short window. Noticed it only for a particular webhook call that sometimes times out, then client retries. asking if this is SDK/undici interplay or our endpoint duplicate processing.

maya: ugh, annoying. do we have a quick repro or logs? which ts-sdk/node versions?

alex: repro attached inline. node v18.16.0, ts-sdk@0.4.2, undici@5.22.1. sample client code they used: 

```js
// simplified repro from customer
async function send(payload) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/events', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(payload),
    signal: controller.signal,
  });
  // naive retry on network errors
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('bad');
}

// on timeout they do: controller.abort(); and then call send(payload) again
```

alex: customer says sometimes both requests arrive at server (same payload id) and billing shows 3 attempts within ~10s.

steve: couple hypotheses: 1) abort timing - undici may not cancel the connection quickly and server still processes body, then client retries; 2) reuse of a readable stream / non-cloneable body leading the SDK to actually reissue a fresh body by accident; 3) retry/backoff logic starting a new attempt before previous socket fully terminated creating a duplicate write on the wire.

steve: can we get raw request IDs from the server side (trace-id) and the client-side request id header if they set one? that'd show whether same TCP/connection produced multiple downstream records.

maya: asked customer for request/trace IDs and billing IDs. also asked if they set an idempotency header. they said no.

alex: they forwarded server logs showing two POST bodies accepted ~1.2s apart, then a third ~6s later. looks like first was processed, second came during retry window.

steve: actionable: recommend they avoid reusing the same AbortController across attempts and create a fresh one per try. also suggest adding an Idempotency-Key header as mitigation. example pattern: 

```js
async function sendWithRetry(payload) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    const controller = new AbortController(); // new per attempt
    try {
      const res = await fetch(url, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload), signal: controller.signal });
      if (res.ok) return res;
    } catch (err) {
      // backoff before next attempt
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100 * Math.pow(2, i)));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('failed after retries');
}
```

steve: also note if payload is a stream (e.g., file) you must buffer or re-create it for each attempt; otherwise the body may be empty or partially resent.

maya: do we want an internal probe to check SDK's retry implementation with undici signals? could be a subtle race where abort handler doesn't wait for socket close before starting next attempt.

steve: yes, i'll run a small harness that aborts mid-request and spikes parallel retries against a local endpoint to see if we can reproduce duplicate server-side deliveries. will report back.

sam-bot: created support ticket #SUP-5421 for billing team and flagged eng for followup :ticket:

alex: customer ok with mitigations (idempotency header + new AbortController per try) but wants root cause. thanks — will wait for your harness results, steve. :thumbsup:
